points all have (d,idx) lexicographically smaller than p. So safe.\n\nThus merge is correct.\n\nNow performance: We need choose scheduling. Since each point computation identical cost, static block schedule with default chunk size should balance across 4 threads. But there may be overhead of per-thread local arrays and merging; for small N maybe not worth it but baseline time 2s suggests large N. Previous best experiment got ~0.026s (score .9876) which is huge speedup (~80x). That likely used some clever algorithm like sorting all distances? Actually KNN with k maybe small, total points maybe millions; parallel distance computation and selection could be fast.\n\nWe can also consider using OpenMP reduction to compute min-heap? But our approach should be good.\n\nLet's write code.\n\nFirst modify knn.h? We may need include <omp.h> in .c only. Also we might want to add a struct for candidate; define in .c.\n\nPotential issue: The function knn currently takes n_groups, groups array, k, point. It allocates labels and distances arrays of size k. We'll replace its body with parallel version.\n\nWe need include <omp.h> at top of knn.c after #include \"knn.h\". Also maybe use qsort from stdlib already included via header? Header includes stdio/stdlib; yes.\n\nImplementation steps in knn:\n\n- Compute total_points = sum over groups lengths.\n- Allocate arrays xs, ys, ls (labels) of size total_points. Fill sequentially:\n  int idx=0;\n  for i in n_groups: for j in g.length: xs[idx]=g.points[j].x; ys[idx]=...; ls[idx]=g.label; idx++.\n\nThis adds O(N) memory and time but negligible compared to distance computations? Actually filling is cheap. Could avoid by using groups directly with nested loops inside parallel region, but then each thread would need to iterate over its assigned range of flattened index and map back to group/point. That mapping requires precomputed offsets per group or binary search; more complex. Pre-filling arrays simplifies.\n\n- Determine max threads: int